-- What a field default will not accept.
--
-- A default is the same kind of thing a global's initializer is - a constant the declaration
-- reduces to, because there is no program point at which a declaration's code would run - and since
-- compiler/resolve/const.cpp it is the same evaluator, so the two positions accept and refuse the
-- same forms. This file is the refusals; Constant.yana is what they both accept.
--
-- The type is not in question here the way it is for a global: a field has one already, so a
-- default is checked against it rather than deciding it.

data Pair {a: Int, b: Int}
data Direction = North | South
data Shape = Circle(Int) | Square(Int)

-- A number that is not one of the type's, in both directions: a decimal for an integer field, and
-- an integer wider than the field can hold.
data Rounded {value: Int = 2.5}
data TooBig {value: U8 = 300}

-- A memory type has no constant form at all, which is the same answer a global of one gets.
data Nested {inner: Pair = 0}

-- An ascription is accepted here as well, and has to agree with the field it is on rather than
-- silently replacing it.
data Ascribed {value: Int = 1 :: I64}

-- An enumeration constant of the wrong enumeration, which is a constructor that is a perfectly good
-- constant of a type this field does not have.
data Mixed {flag: Bool = North}

-- A constructor that carries a value is storage rather than a number, whatever it is written in
-- front of.
data Held {shape: Shape = Circle(1)}

-- A name that is no constructor at all.
data Unknown {heading: Direction = West}

-- And an expression, which is the form neither position has: there is nothing to evaluate it at.
data Computed {value: Int = 1 + 1}

-- A field whose own type did not resolve is reported once, by the type resolution that failed. A
-- default is not then evaluated against the error type it recovered with.
data Broken {value: Nope = 0}

fn main() -> Int = 0
